From: mx-kernel
Subject: [PATCH] iopub_welcome: send {} rather than null for parent_header/metadata

xpublisher::create_xpub_message builds an xmessage_base_data without
initialising m_parent_header or m_metadata. nl::json default-constructs to
null, so the iopub_welcome message published at startup goes onto the wire
with:

    parent_header: null
    metadata: null

The Jupyter messaging specification requires an empty object when a message
has no parent:

    https://jupyter-client.readthedocs.io/en/latest/messaging.html#parent-header
    "If there is no parent, an empty dict should be used."

Clients that follow the spec and call .get() on parent_header therefore fail
against any xeus-zmq kernel. jupyter-console 6.6.3 raises

    Exception 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'get'

from ptshell.py:825 as soon as it connects. JupyterLab tolerates it, which is
why the problem looks client-specific at first.

Initialising both fields to nl::json::object() fixes it at the source.

Applies to: xeus-zmq 3.1.1
Upstream: not yet reported -- see patches/README.md

--- a/src/server/xpublisher.cpp
+++ b/src/server/xpublisher.cpp
@@ -41,6 +41,18 @@
     {
         xmessage_base_data data;
         data.m_header = xeus::make_header("iopub_welcome", "", "");
+        // LOCAL PATCH (mx-kernel) -- see patches/README.md and
+        // patches/xeus-zmq-0001-iopub-welcome-parent-header.patch
+        //
+        // nl::json default-constructs to null, not {}. Serialising null here
+        // puts `null` on the wire for parent_header, and the Jupyter messaging
+        // spec requires an empty object when a message has no parent. Clients
+        // that call .get() on it (jupyter-console does) then raise
+        // "'NoneType' object has no attribute 'get'" on connect.
+        //
+        // Do not drop these two lines when refreshing the vendored xeus-zmq.
+        data.m_parent_header = nl::json::object();
+        data.m_metadata = nl::json::object();
         data.m_content["subscription"] = topic;
         xpub_message p_msg("", std::move(data));
 
